Question 3
	The drawbacks of this approach would be:
	There is tight coupling to StoredSequenceContext so any change made inside this class breaks the adapter and anything that
	depends on it.
	The adapter inherits protected and public members of StoredSequenceContext and this weakens encapsulation.

	The client now depends on SequenceListStore interface and StoredSequenceContext class.
	So the client is indirectly coupled to an implementation detail.

Question 4
	The object adapter, ComStoredSequenceListAdapter, uses composition instead of inheritance.
	It’s more flexible because you can change or replace StoredSequenceContext without breaking the Client.
	The Client now depends only on the SequenceListStore interface, not on the concrete StoredSequenceContext, 
	so coupling is reduced and encapsulation is stronger.
	
	


